.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
Handle table: opaque 64 bit identity with stale handle detection\&. 

Internal header\&. Not part of the public ABI, so the struct is visible here and callers may place a table on the stack or embed it in another struct\&.
.PP
The table maps opaque 64 bit handles to void pointers\&. Each slot carries a generation counter that is bumped when the slot is released, so a handle to a released object is rejected rather than resolving to whatever now occupies the slot\&. That check is what makes use after free a returned error instead of memory corruption, which matters when two language runtimes are calling in\&.
.PP
\fBNot part of the host facing interface\&.\fP How the toolkit identifies objects\&. A host binds to \fBschultz_api\&.h\fP; this header is the toolkit's own and may change without notice\&. 
.PP
Definition in file \fBschultz_handle\&.h\fP\&.
.SH "Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "int32_t schultz_handle_table_init (\fBschultz_handle_table\fP * table, uint32_t initial_capacity)"

.PP
Prepares a table for use\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItable\fP The table to initialize\&. Must not be NULL\&. Its previous contents are overwritten, so call schultz_handle_table_free first if it was in use\&. 
.br
\fIinitial_capacity\fP Slots to allocate up front\&. Pass 0 to accept the default\&. The table grows on demand regardless\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK on success, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when table is NULL or initial_capacity exceeds the maximum, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY when the slot array could not be allocated\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void schultz_handle_table_free (\fBschultz_handle_table\fP * table)"

.PP
Releases a table's storage\&. The objects the table pointed at are not touched; ownership of those belongs to the caller\&. The table is left safe to call again and safe to pass to schultz_handle_table_init\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItable\fP The table to release\&. NULL and an already freed table are both accepted and do nothing\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_handle_table_insert (\fBschultz_handle_table\fP * table, void * object, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_handle)"

.PP
Stores an object and issues a handle for it\&. Reuses a free slot when one exists, otherwise grows the table\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItable\fP The table to insert into\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIobject\fP The pointer to store\&. Must not be NULL, because NULL is how a released slot is recognized\&. The table does not take ownership and never dereferences it\&. 
.br
\fIout_handle\fP Receives the new handle on success\&. Must not be NULL\&. Left untouched on failure\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK on success, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when any pointer is NULL, SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY when growth failed, or SCHULTZ_ERR_EXHAUSTED when the index space is used up\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_handle_table_lookup (const \fBschultz_handle_table\fP * table, \fBschultz_handle\fP handle, void ** out_object)"

.PP
Resolves a handle to the object it names\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItable\fP The table to look in\&. NULL yields SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE rather than a crash\&. 
.br
\fIhandle\fP The handle to resolve\&. 
.br
\fIout_object\fP Receives the stored pointer on success\&. Must not be NULL\&. Left untouched on failure\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK on success, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when out_object is NULL, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the handle is zero, out of range, names a free slot, or carries a stale generation\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_handle_table_remove (\fBschultz_handle_table\fP * table, \fBschultz_handle\fP handle)"

.PP
Releases the slot a handle names\&. Bumps the slot's generation, which invalidates this handle and every copy of it\&. The slot is returned to the free list for reuse\&. The object itself is not freed; that is the caller's business\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItable\fP The table to remove from\&. NULL yields SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE rather than a crash\&. 
.br
\fIhandle\fP The handle to release\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK on success, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the handle is not currently valid, which includes releasing twice\&. 
.RE
.PP

